UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 15, Lesson 1: Performing and reflecting', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Performing drama for an audience means projecting voice to the back without shouting, holding stillness when listening and not blocking sightlines. If something goes wrong, stay in character and improvise back to the story. Afterwards reflect with evidence: what objective was clear, where tension peaked.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nList three onstage etiquette rules and one recovery idea for a forgotten line.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Face audience diagonals, listen actively, no chatting offstage. If a line is lost, paraphrase the objective until a partner’s cue returns.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nLaughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPerform your play or scene for family. Complete a reflection: clearest objective, strongest tension moment, one etiquette win, one fix.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Performing drama for an audience means projecting voice to the back without shouting, holding stillness when listening and not blocking sightlines. If something goes wrong, stay in character and improvise back to the story. Afterwards reflect with evidence: what objective was clear, where tension peaked.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Perform your play or scene for family. Complete a reflection: clearest objective, strongest tension moment, one etiquette win, one fix. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Face audience diagonals, listen actively, no chatting offstage. If a line is lost, paraphrase the objective until a partner’s cue returns.'' Reasoning: I used performance etiquette by attending to projection rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Laughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate. Correction: Strong answer: ''Face audience diagonals, listen actively, no chatting offstage. If a line is lost, paraphrase the objective until a partner’s cue returns.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of performance etiquette.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Laughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating performance etiquette on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with performance etiquette, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass"