 for an audience means projecting voice to the back without shouting, holding stillness when… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Laughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate.","Perform your play or scene for family. Complete a reflection: clearest objective, strongest tension moment, one etiquette win, one fix.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of performance etiquette?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating performance etiquette on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Face audience diagonals, listen actively, no chatting offstage. If a line is lost, paraphrase the objective until a partner’s cue returns.'' Reasoning: I used performance etiquette by attending to projection rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception 