best shows accurate use of this idea about performance etiquette?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: During a dropped prop, the actor picks it up in character as if it belonged in the moment, then continues. Reflection: ''Objective clear in scene 2; climax rushed—need a pause.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nList three onstage etiquette rules and one recovery idea for a forgotten line.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Face audience diagonals, listen actively, no chatting offstage. If a line is lost, paraphrase the objective until a partner’s cue returns.''","explain":"Performing drama for an audience means projecting voice to the back without shouting, holding stillness when listening and not blocking sightlines. If something goes wrong, stay in character and improvise back to the story. Afterwards reflect with evidence: what objective was clear, where tension peaked.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Laughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Performance craft.","caption":"Be seen and heard.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\