 at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Laughing at mistakes onstage. Support the story first; laugh later offstage if appropriate. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about performance etiquette?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPerforming drama for an audience means projecting voice to the back without shouting, holding stillness when listening and not blocking sightlines. If something goes wrong, stay in character and improvise back to the story. Afterwards reflect with evidence: what objective was clear, where tension peaked. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 15, Lesson 1: Performing and reflecting","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Improvisation and playbuilding","week":15,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: During a dropped prop, the actor picks it up in character as if it belonged in the moment, then continues. Reflection: ''Objective clear in scene 2; climax rushed—need a pause.''\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Performance craft.","caption":"Be seen and heard.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Project</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">voice to back</text><text x=\